close_range for the child of a subprocess, on Radix

A child that subprocess starts closes every descriptor but those it was
asked to keep. CPython does that with close_range only where it knows the
function to be async-signal-safe, which it takes to be so on Linux and
FreeBSD; elsewhere it lists /proc/self/fd, which Radix does not have, and
then closes each number up to sysconf(_SC_OPEN_MAX), the soft
RLIMIT_NOFILE. A descriptor above a limit that was lowered stays open in
the child (test_close_fds_when_max_fd_is_lowered). Radix's close_range
takes no lock and allocates nothing: it clears bits of the C library's
table of descriptors and calls the kernel for each descriptor it closes,
so it is as safe between fork and exec as close is.

--- a/Modules/_posixsubprocess.c
+++ b/Modules/_posixsubprocess.c
@@ -562,9 +562,12 @@
  *
  * On Linux, glibc explicitly documents it to be a thin wrapper over
  * the system call, and other C libraries are likely to follow glibc.
+ * Radix's C library does it with atomic operations on its table of
+ * descriptors and a kernel call for each one it closes: no lock, no
+ * allocation.
  */
 #if defined(HAVE_CLOSE_RANGE) && \
-    (defined(__linux__) || defined(__FreeBSD__))
+    (defined(__linux__) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__radix__))
 #define HAVE_ASYNC_SAFE_CLOSE_RANGE
 
 static int
